1. Kubernetes Networking:
    - Kubernetes networking is about how traffic flows:
        Into the cluster → external users accessing apps
        Inside the cluster → Pods talking to each other
        Out of the cluster → apps reaching databases/APIs
    - The three concepts you asked about solve different layers of networking:
        Concept	        Main Purpose	                            Scope
        Ingress	        HTTP/HTTPS traffic routing into cluster	    North-South traffic
        Gateway API	    Modern replacement/evolution of Ingress	    Advanced traffic management
        NetworkPolicy	Control Pod-to-Pod communication	        East-West security

2. What is Ingress:
    - An Ingress is a Kubernetes API object that manages external HTTP/HTTPS access to services inside the cluster.
    - It acts like a smart reverse proxy/router.
    - Instead of exposing every service with a LoadBalancer, you expose one entry point and route traffic internally.
    - Basic Flow
        Internet User
            |
            v
        Ingress Controller
            |
            +----> Service A ---> Pods
            |
            +----> Service B ---> Pods
    - Important distinction:
        Ingress resource = configuration
        Ingress Controller = actual software implementing it
    - Common controllers:
        NGINX Ingress Controller
        Traefik
        HAProxy Ingress
        Istio
    - Example Use Case:
        Domain          Service
        app.example.com	frontend
        api.example.com	backend API
        - Ingress routes requests based on hostname/path.

3. What Ingress Can Do:
    - Host-based routing
        app.example.com -> frontend
        api.example.com -> backend
    - Path-based routing
        /example.com/api -> api-service
        /example.com/web -> web-service
    - TLS termination
    - Handles HTTPS certificates.
        tls:
        - hosts:
            - app.example.com
            secretName: tls-secret
    - Load balancing
        Distributes traffic across Pods.
    - Ingress Architecture
        Client
            |
            v
        Cloud Load Balancer
            |
            v
        Ingress Controller Pod
            |
            v
        Kubernetes Service
            |
            v
        Pods
    - Limitations of Ingress
    - Ingress became too limited for modern needs:
        Mostly HTTP/HTTPS only
        Different controllers support different annotations
        No standard advanced traffic policies
        Hard to extend
        Vendor-specific features everywhere
    - This led to the creation of Gateway API.

4. What is Gateway API:
    - Gateway API is the next-generation Kubernetes traffic management API.
    - It is designed to replace many limitations of Ingress.
    - Think of it as:
        Ingress 2.0
    - It is:
        More expressive
        More modular
        More role-oriented
        Easier to extend
        Protocol-aware
    - Managed by the Kubernetes SIG Network community.
    - Core Gateway API Resources
        Resource	    Purpose
        GatewayClass	Defines implementation
        Gateway	        Entry point/load balancer
        HTTPRoute	    HTTP routing rules
        TCPRoute	    TCP traffic rules
        GRPCRoute	    gRPC routing
        TLSRoute	    TLS passthrough
    - Gateway API Architecture
        Internet
            |
            v
        Gateway
            |
            +---- HTTPRoute ---> Service A
            |
            +---- HTTPRoute ---> Service B
    - Unlike Ingress, routing is separated cleanly.
    - Example Gateway API
        - Gateway
            apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
            kind: Gateway
            metadata:
                name: shared-gateway
            spec:
                gatewayClassName: nginx
                listeners:
                - name: http
                    protocol: HTTP
                    port: 80
        - HTTPRoute
            apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
            kind: HTTPRoute
            metadata:
                name: app-route
            spec:
                parentRefs:
                - name: shared-gateway
                hostnames:
                - app.example.com
                rules:
                - matches:
                    - path:
                        type: PathPrefix
                        value: /
                    backendRefs:
                    - name: frontend-service
                    port: 80

5. Why Gateway API is Better:
    1. Separation of Responsibilities
    - Different teams can own different layers.
        Team	        Resource
        Platform Team	Gateway
        App Team	    HTTPRoute
    - Very important in enterprises.
    2. Better Traffic Management
        - Supports:
            traffic splitting
            canary deployments
            header matching
            retries
            timeouts
            mirroring
        - Example:
            90% traffic -> v1
            10% traffic -> v2
    3. Multi-Protocol Support
        - Ingress:
            Mainly HTTP/HTTPS
        - Gateway API:
            HTTP
            HTTPS
            TCP
            UDP
            gRPC
            TLS
    4. Portable Standard
    - Ingress relied heavily on annotations:
        annotations:
            nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
    - Gateway API standardizes many such features.

6. Ingress vs Gateway API:
    Feature	            Ingress	        Gateway API
    Simplicity	        Simple	        More advanced
    Protocol support	Mostly HTTP	    Multi-protocol
    Extensibility	    Limited	        High
    Role separation	    Weak	        Strong
    Traffic policies	Basic	        Advanced
    Future direction	Legacy-ish	    Kubernetes future
    - Use Ingress if:
        Small/simple setup
        Basic HTTP routing
        Quick deployments
        Existing mature setup
    - Use Gateway API if:
        Large platforms
        Multi-team environments
        Advanced traffic management
        gRPC/TCP needs
        Long-term architecture

7. What is NetworkPolicy:
    - A NetworkPolicy controls which Pods can communicate with other Pods.
    - It is basically a Kubernetes firewall.
    - Without NetworkPolicy:
        Every Pod can talk to every other Pod
    - This is usually insecure.
    
    - Why NetworkPolicy Matters
        - Imagine: frontend -> backend -> database
        - You want:
            frontend can call backend
            backend can call database
            frontend CANNOT directly call database
        NetworkPolicy enforces this.
        - Traffic Types
            - Ingress Traffic
            Traffic coming into a Pod.
                Other Pod ---> Target Pod
            - Egress Traffic
            Traffic leaving a Pod.
                Target Pod ---> External/Internal destination
        - Example NetworkPolicy
        - Allow frontend to access backend
            apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
            kind: NetworkPolicy
            metadata:
                name: backend-policy
            spec:
                podSelector:
                    matchLabels:
                        app: backend
                policyTypes:
                    - Ingress
                ingress:
                - from:
                    - podSelector:
                        matchLabels:
                            app: frontend
        - Meaning: Only frontend Pods can access backend Pods

    - Default Behavior
        - Without NetworkPolicy
            ALLOW ALL
        - With NetworkPolicy
        Once a Pod is selected by a policy:
            DENY ALL except explicitly allowed
        This is critical.
        Default Deny Example
            apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
            kind: NetworkPolicy
            metadata:
                name: default-deny
            spec:
                podSelector: {}
                policyTypes:
                - Ingress
        This blocks all incoming traffic to all Pods in the namespace.

    - Real-World Zero Trust Model
        - Typical production setup:
            Internet
                |
            Ingress/Gateway
                |
            Frontend Pods
                |
            Backend Pods
                |
            Database Pods
        - With NetworkPolicies:
            frontend only talks to backend
            backend only talks to DB
            monitoring allowed selectively
            DNS explicitly allowed
            everything else denied

    - Important Limitation
        - NetworkPolicy only works if your CNI plugin supports it.
        - Supported CNIs include:
            Calico
            Cilium
            Antrea
        - Basic CNIs may ignore policies.
    
    - How These Concepts Work Together
        - Full Traffic Flow
                External Traffic
                       |
                       v
              Ingress / Gateway API
                       |
                       v
                 Kubernetes Services
                       |
                       v
                      Pods
                       |
            NetworkPolicy controls access
        - Simple Mental Model
            Ingress: "How does outside traffic enter my cluster?"
            Gateway API: "How do I manage modern, advanced traffic routing?"
            NetworkPolicy: "Which Pods are allowed to talk to which Pods?"
    
    - Practical Example
        - Imagine an e-commerce system:
        Component	        Role
        Gateway API	        Routes customer traffic
        frontend service	UI
        payment service	    Payment logic
        database	        Stores orders
        NetworkPolicy	    Prevents unauthorized communication

    - Flow:
        User
            |
        Gateway
            |
        Frontend
            |
        Payment Service
            |
        Database
    
    - Policies ensure:
        frontend cannot access DB directly
        only payment service accesses DB
        only gateway exposes frontend